\pard\qc\b\f1\fs40\cf2 Proposal: Composite Psychosocial Risk Indices for Leakage-Free Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh\b0\fs22\cf1\f0\par
\pard\qc\i\fs20\cf3 A Feature-Engineering and Explainable-AI Research Proposal\line Prepared by: Samir\line Dataset: Mendeley "Data for Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh" (n = 800)\line Date: 27 July 2026\i0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa200\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 1. Background and Motivation\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Postpartum Depression (PPD) affects a significant proportion of mothers in Bangladesh, yet clinical screening instruments such as the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS) and the Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ-9) require a trained interviewer to administer a full multi-item questionnaire at each visit. In many low-resource settings, this creates a practical bottleneck: not every mother can be screened with the full instrument at every contact point.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This proposal outlines a plan to investigate whether PPD risk can instead be estimated from background information that is already routinely collected or easy to ask about - demographic, economic, family, pregnancy, and neonatal circumstances - without requiring the EPDS/PHQ-9 items themselves. If feasible, such a model could serve as a lightweight first-pass screening aid, helping prioritize which mothers should receive the full clinical assessment first.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The proposed work will use the publicly available Mendeley "Data for Postpartum Depression Prediction in Bangladesh" dataset (n = 800), which includes both the full EPDS/PHQ-9 responses and around 50 additional background questions per mother.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 2. Problem Statement: The Data Leakage Risk in Existing Approaches\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A review of the literature associated with this dataset raises an important methodological concern. Several existing studies in this space predict the EPDS/PHQ-9-derived outcome label using the EPDS/PHQ-9 item answers (or their raw summed scores) as input features. Since the label is arithmetically derived from those same items, this constitutes data leakage - the model is not learning a genuine risk profile, it is simply being handed the answer in a different form. One external reference paper in this area reports an implausibly high ~0.9999 AUROC, a result that is almost certainly explained by this leakage pattern rather than genuine predictive signal.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A core design principle of this proposal is therefore to strictly exclude every EPDS/PHQ-9 item and raw score from the model's input features, at every stage of the pipeline, for every candidate target variable. Only the ~53 background ("safe") questions will be used as predictors. This constraint is necessary for a trustworthy model, but it also raises the central question this proposal addresses: if individual background answers are, on their own, comparatively weak predictors, can they be combined more intelligently to recover useful signal?\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 3. Proposed Novel Contribution: Composite Psychosocial Risk Indices\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.1 What We Propose to Build\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Rather than feeding all ~53 raw background answers into a model as isolated, independent columns, this proposal introduces four clinically-motivated composite indices, each intended to combine several related raw survey answers into a single interpretable 0-10 score:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 1.\tab \b Economic Stability Index (ESI)\b0  - intended to capture overall economic stability, combining: Current monthly income, Husband's monthly income, Occupation after latest childbirth, Education level.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 2.\tab \b Social/Family Support Index (SSI)\b0  - intended to capture the level of family and social support available to the mother, combining: Family type (joint/nuclear), Relationship with in-laws, Relationship with husband, Number of household members, Received Support level.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 3.\tab \b Maternal Mental-Health Risk Index (MHRI)\b0  - intended to capture cumulative psychosocial risk, combining: Depression before pregnancy (PHQ-2 screen), Depression during pregnancy (PHQ-2 screen), Disease before pregnancy, Abuse history, History of pregnancy loss.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 4.\tab \b Neonatal/Delivery Stress Index (NSI)\b0  - intended to capture delivery- and newborn-related stress, combining: Mode of delivery, Birth complications, Newborn illness, Worry about the newborn.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This design is intended to mirror standard clinical practice: EPDS and PHQ-9 themselves are composite scores, built by summing multiple related item answers into a single interpretable number rather than treating each item in isolation. The proposal aims to apply this same logic to the "safe" (non-leakage) side of the dataset, on the hypothesis that a mother's overall support/risk profile may be more predictive than any single background answer alone.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.2 Proposed Construction Methodology\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Each composite index is planned to be built in four steps:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 1.\tab \b Ordinal mapping\b0 : assign each raw sub-feature's categories a numeric score along a clinically reasoned order (for example, income brackets ordered from lowest to highest).\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 2.\tab \b Empirical direction validation\b0 : rather than assuming the clinically "obvious" direction is always correct, the plan is to check each mapping's assumed direction against the actual observed PPD rate per category in the data, and to revise any mapping the data contradicts. This guards against silently encoding an incorrect assumption into the index.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 3.\tab \b Min-max normalization\b0 : rescale each mapped sub-feature to a common [0, 1] range so that sub-features measured on different original scales can be combined fairly.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 4.\tab \b Weighted averaging and scaling\b0 : combine the normalized sub-features into a single 0-10 score.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 3.3 Why a Weighted Approach Is Proposed\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A simple equal-weight average across an index's sub-features risks a specific failure mode: if one sub-feature (for example, a history of abuse) is a much stronger individual predictor than the others in its group, equal averaging can dilute that strong signal with noisier, weaker sub-features, potentially making the resulting composite index less useful than its single strongest component.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 To avoid this, the proposal is to weight each sub-feature's contribution to its composite index by its own measured association strength with the primary clinical outcome (PPD_binary) - specifically, -log10(p) from a chi-square test, normalized so weights sum to 1. This is intended to be a data-driven, reproducible weighting scheme rather than a manual or arbitrary adjustment, and the plan is to compare both equal-weight and weighted variants to confirm the weighting genuinely helps before finalizing the approach.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 4. Proposed Validation Plan\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 To ensure the composite indices' value is demonstrated rigorously rather than assumed, this proposal plans three independent, complementary validation methods, so that no single conclusion rests on one potentially misleading metric.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.1 Statistical Association Testing\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Each composite index will be tested against all three candidate targets (EPDS Result, PHQ-9 Result, PPD_binary) using ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis tests, to establish whether each index carries a statistically significant univariate signal about PPD risk on its own, before any modeling is attempted.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.2 Ablation Study\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 A systematic ablation study is planned to isolate the composite indices' actual contribution, comparing three progressively richer feature sets under an identical modeling and cross-validation protocol:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm A "Raw only"\b0 : all raw background features, no composite indices.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm B "Raw + Composite"\b0 : Arm A's features plus the composite indices.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Arm C "Selected + Composite"\b0 : the most important features (by model-based importance) selected from Arm B's pool, to test whether a smaller, cleaner feature set can match or exceed the full set.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Feature selection for Arm C is planned to be performed strictly within each cross-validation fold's training portion only, to avoid any risk of the test data influencing which features are selected (a subtle form of data leakage sometimes called "double-dipping"). Paired statistical significance testing (t-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank test across folds) is also planned, to check whether any observed differences between arms are statistically distinguishable from random variation rather than relying on point-estimate comparisons alone.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 4.3 SHAP Explainability\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 As a third, independent line of evidence, this proposal plans to compute SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values for the selected model, evaluated on a held-out test set the model has not seen during training. This method quantifies, for each individual prediction, how much each feature pushed the prediction toward or away from higher PPD risk, and will be used to check whether the composite indices genuinely rank among the most influential features from the model's own perspective - not just in aggregate statistical tests.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 5. Proposed Modeling Approach\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.1 Baseline Models\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 The plan is to train and compare five model families for each target, using stratified 5-fold cross-validation plus an 80/20 holdout split: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost, each with class-balanced weighting to address the mild class imbalance present in the targets.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.2 Advanced Modeling: Tuning, Deep Learning, and a Hybrid Ensemble\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 To ensure the baseline comparison is fair and thorough, three further avenues are planned:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b Hyperparameter tuning\b0  via RandomizedSearchCV for the tree-based models, to check whether tuning meaningfully improves on default settings.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b A Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)\b0  as a deep-learning comparison point. Given the modest sample size (n = 800), the expectation going in is that a deep network is unlikely to outperform gradient boosting on this kind of small tabular dataset - a well-established pattern in the machine learning literature - but this will be tested empirically rather than assumed, including logging how many training epochs the network actually uses before early stopping, as a diagnostic for underfitting versus adequate capacity.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab \b A Stacking Ensemble\b0 , combining several tuned tree-based models with a Logistic Regression meta-learner, to test whether a genuine hybrid model can outperform any single model family.\par

\pard\sb160\sa100\b\f1\fs24\cf2 5.3 Proposed Model Choice for the Explainability Analysis\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 For the SHAP explainability analysis specifically, the plan is to use a single Random Forest model rather than a more complex ensemble such as the Stacking model, even if the ensemble scores marginally higher. The reasoning is that SHAP's TreeExplainer method provides fast, mathematically exact Shapley values for a single tree-based ensemble, whereas explaining a heterogeneous stacked model would require a slower, approximate method and would produce a less clear, less defensible interpretability narrative. Since the goal of the explainability analysis is transparent, patient-level interpretation, this proposal prioritizes clarity of explanation over the last one to two percentage points of raw accuracy a more complex ensemble might offer.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 6. Expected Outcomes and Significance\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 If the proposed composite indices perform as hypothesized, the expected outcomes are: (a) each composite index shows a statistically significant association with PPD risk on its own; (b) models trained on Raw+Composite features outperform models trained on raw features alone, and models using a selected subset of Raw+Composite features perform comparably or better with fewer inputs; (c) SHAP analysis shows the composite indices ranking among the most influential features for the model's predictions. For the binary PPD screening target specifically, the aim is to reach a level of discriminative performance (ROC-AUC) broadly comparable to published leakage-free PPD screening models in the literature, which typically report AUC in the 0.65-0.85 range.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Beyond raw performance, the methodological contribution this proposal aims to demonstrate is a reproducible process: a strict, auditable leakage-free protocol; composite feature engineering that is empirically validated and corrected rather than assumed; a data-driven weighting scheme for combining sub-features; proof of the composite features' value through multiple independent, complementary evidence streams; and transparent, patient-level explainability via a model chosen deliberately for interpretability.\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Clinically, if the hypothesis holds, this would suggest that in this Bangladeshi cohort, family/social support adequacy and post-childbirth emotional response are more informative of PPD risk than economic circumstances alone - a pattern that would be consistent with existing literature on postpartum mental health in South Asian family structures, where relational dynamics with in-laws and spouse are often emphasized over purely material factors.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 7. Anticipated Limitations\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 Several limitations are anticipated and will be addressed transparently regardless of outcome:\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab Sample size (n = 800) is modest, particularly once split across the five PHQ-9 severity bands, which may leave some bands with relatively few mothers.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab With only five cross-validation folds, paired statistical significance testing for the ablation study will have limited statistical power; a small but real effect may not reach conventional significance thresholds, and this will be reported honestly rather than overstated.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab The real-world meaning of certain survey items (for example, the exact phrasing of the "Abuse" question) may need to be verified against the original Bangla questionnaire wording if any counter-intuitive patterns emerge during validation.\par
\pard\li420\fi-420\sa100\fs22 -\tab EPDS Result and PHQ-9 Result are ordinal (ordered) categories; this proposal initially plans to model them as standard multi-class targets, with an ordinal-aware approach identified as a possible future extension.\par

\pard\sb200\sa120\b\f1\fs28\cf2 8. Conclusion\b0\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa160\fs22 This proposal outlines a plan to engineer and rigorously validate four composite psychosocial risk indices for leakage-free postpartum depression screening in a Bangladeshi cohort, combined with a systematic modeling and explainability plan to test whether these engineered features add genuine, defensible predictive value over raw survey responses alone. The intended contribution is not a single performance number but a transparent, reproducible methodology - one that would be honestly reported, including its limitations, regardless of whether every hypothesis is ultimately confirmed.\par
}
